  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nHappy September 1st and start to meteorological fall! Summer shows \nno signs of slowing down as we start September, with an expansive \nupper level ridge overhead expected to bring hot and humid \nconditions through much of this week. A Heat Advisory remains in \neffect for the entire forecast area through 8 PM EDT Wednesday. Our \nlate summer/early fall heatwave brings high temperatures in the \nupper 80s to low 90s through at least midweek. With return flow off \nof the Gulf due to surface high pressure centered over the southeast \nCONUS, high humidity is expected to persist through at least \nmidweek. Despite the unexpected showers and storms this morning,\nclearing is already ongoing this afternoon per visible \nsatellite imagery, which will allow for temperatures to climb \ninto at least the mid to upper 80s for highs today Slightly \nwarmer Wednesday with highs in the low 90s with the peak of the \nridge overhead. Heat indices are expected to be at or slightly \nabove 100 degrees, especially this afternoon and again Wednesday\nafternoon. There will be minimal relief overnight with near \nrecord lows in the mid 70s. Heat indices around 100 degrees may \npersist along and south of US 30 into Thursday, so the Heat \nAdvisory may need extended in a future forecast update. \n\nConsiderable uncertainty remains in regards to rain and storms \nchances this week. All models have struggled with this pattern \nover the past few days; a majority of recent runs of hi-res \nguidance have been misleading, keeping the upper level ridge \nwell to our north whereas in reality, our area (especially the \nnorthern half) has been on that periphery as well. We had \nscattered showers and storms in the vicinity of the US 30 \ncorridor this morning and none of the models showed anything \noccuring. Models have been taking the rain/storm chances north \nout of our area, which has created challenges and considerable \nuncertainty in regards to what to do with PoPs over the next few\ndays. The NBM wants to hold off on any rain for our Lower \nMichigan counties until early Thursday at the earliest. However,\nI cannot rule out at least isolated shower/storm development in\nour forecast area (especially north of I-80/I-90) tonight and \nWednesday, where I have added broad 15-20% PoPs. Should any \nstorms develop, heat and humidity should fuel MLCAPE values of \nup to 3000-4000 J/kg and steeping low level lapse rates. SPC \ndoes have at least the northern/northeast half of our area in a \nMarginal Risk each day through Thursday with the main risk being\ngusty to damaging winds. Confidence is low given the \naforementioned uncertainty with models. Better chances (30-50%) \nfor rain/storms area- wide are in the forecast for later \nThursday into Friday as the upper level ridge gradually \nflattens, although uncertainty still remains in the forecast \nseveral days out given the recent unreliable model guidance. Our\nfar southwest counties have the best chances for remaining \ncompletely dry this week with heat and humidity continuing \nthrough late in the week. By the weekend, temperatures trend \ncloser towards normal with highs in the low 80s.",
